    "case_id": "184",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 50s with a 12-year history of recurrent follicular lymphoma presented with a subacute, multifocal, pruritic, painful eruption that began on the abdomen and then involved the scalp, groin, and extremities. He denied fevers, chills, weight loss, or other systemic symptoms. His medications included oxycodone, duvelisib (a PI3K inhibitor), and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, all started 14 months earlier for lymphoma management. On examination, there were well-demarcated erythematous plaques on the abdomen, axillae, and inguinal folds; scattered erythematous scaly papules and plaques on the scalp, extremities, and hands; and confluent erythematous hyperkeratotic plaques with plate-like scales on the plantar feet. Two skin biopsies (abdomen and left foot) showed parakeratosis with collections of neutrophils, hypogranulosis, regular epidermal hyperplasia with elongated rete ridges, thin suprapapillary plates, dilated papillary dermal vessels, and dermal eosinophils, findings consistent with a psoriasiform drug reaction.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Psoriasiform drug eruption\nB. Duvelisib\nC. TMP\nD. Pityriasis rubra pilaris\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
